{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 485 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "db9948dc7cdd834a1f2fb430a7f21a9433a2d8c642b053e378cbc05a764d3185" :edb/db-before-t 484 :edb/db-after-t 485 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533797 50 #inst "2026-10-05T00:42:08.465Z" 13194139533797 true] [17592186175847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251801 13194139533797 true] [17592186175847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251808 13194139533797 true] [17592186175847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251811 13194139533797 true] [17592186175848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251815 13194139533797 true] [17592186175848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251818 13194139533797 true] [17592186251801 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251802 13194139533797 true] [17592186251801 1089 "q-23453" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251801 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533797 true] [17592186251801 1100 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,7)$ and $(-5,-1).$" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251801 1113 #uuid "7a49d589-0878-5d6a-8a3a-b45fd58a821d" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251801 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,7)$ and $(-5,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-7}{-5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{4}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251803 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251804 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251805 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251806 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251807 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1041 "selection" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251804 13194139533797 true] [17592186251802 1110 #uuid "d2c926c0-3314-53bc-b415-c72ebd191bc4" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251803 1034 "y=\\frac{4}{3}x-3" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251803 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251803 1111 #uuid "832eb1ab-c18a-5073-bdb0-61fe97702c27" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251804 1034 "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x-3" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251804 1111 #uuid "b091fcc5-c198-5dc9-97ff-b667c615d098" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251805 1034 "y=\\frac{7}{3}x-3" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251805 1111 #uuid "c0757b90-64fa-5fa1-b3d0-d3a12bc3032a" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251806 1034 "y=5x-3" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251806 1111 #uuid "81d6c100-d220-56fb-ab1a-160eb7980729" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251807 1034 "y=-2x-3" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251807 1111 #uuid "bc0c2240-a53d-5e1a-930d-570a4a151c13" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251808 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251809 13194139533797 true] [17592186251808 1089 "q-237036" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251808 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533797 true] [17592186251808 1100 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-5,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,-3)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251808 1113 #uuid "3f566655-dd7d-51d9-8487-485b9ec6f512" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251808 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,-3)$ and $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-3)}{2-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-5,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-1(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =-1(x+5) \\\\ y-5 & =-x-5 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251810 13194139533797 true] [17592186251809 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533797 true] [17592186251809 1041 "field-1" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251809 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251810 13194139533797 true] [17592186251809 1110 #uuid "2117ce7d-1c6a-51fe-ba2c-2bf3b5c67ed0" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251810 1034 "-x" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251810 1111 #uuid "b92f57c0-4b73-585e-80a7-ea74c2a224ff" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251811 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251812 13194139533797 true] [17592186251811 1089 "q-237225" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251811 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533797 true] [17592186251811 1100 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-3)$ and $(3,4)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251811 1113 #uuid "1691ebf2-6f4f-5c30-9c4c-95bf9a2f7b40" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251811 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-3)$ and $(3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-3)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{7}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =-\\frac{2}{7}(x-0) \\\\ y+5 & =-\\frac{2}{7}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{7}x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251813 13194139533797 true] [17592186251812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251814 13194139533797 true] [17592186251812 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533797 true] [17592186251812 1041 "field-1" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251812 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251813 13194139533797 true] [17592186251812 1110 #uuid "623389b2-907f-54eb-9303-12b7d106daea" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251813 1034 "-\\frac{2}{7}x-5" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251813 1111 #uuid "a6d50ee8-6512-5a7d-be1a-d3e992691f88" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251814 1034 "\\frac{2}{7}x-5" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251814 1111 #uuid "82ad23ad-a032-538b-ae87-75dc227b52d7" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251815 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251816 13194139533797 true] [17592186251815 1089 "q-237232" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251815 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533797 true] [17592186251815 1100 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,1)$ and is perpendicular to $4x-3y=7?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251815 1113 #uuid "3271c42e-2ceb-5a5e-8cc5-39ef2694e295" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251815 1145 "First, we write the equation $4x-3y=7$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3y & =7 \\\\ -3y & =-4x+7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{4}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251817 13194139533797 true] [17592186251816 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533797 true] [17592186251816 1041 "field-1" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251816 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251817 13194139533797 true] [17592186251816 1110 #uuid "707ba164-f546-5912-b191-c99154f60ca9" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251817 1034 "-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251817 1111 #uuid "6f807f6a-1141-5a12-ad98-14855e6ff19d" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251818 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251819 13194139533797 true] [17592186251818 1089 "q-72571" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251818 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533797 true] [17592186251818 1100 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251818 1113 #uuid "5763625a-277c-5ac6-a65c-0e3abf1f16b7" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251818 1145 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-5}{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(5,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-5) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x+y & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}x+y) & =3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 2x+3y & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251820 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251821 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251822 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251823 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251824 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1041 "selection" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251821 13194139533797 true] [17592186251819 1110 #uuid "73e2ab4f-a2b9-5a10-b72f-a0119678540e" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251820 1034 "2x+3y=7" 13194139533797 true] [17592186251820 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533797 true] [17592186251820 1111 #uuid "68f376a2-d074-5edd-b55e-76566